            "result_text": "Excellent! The regex has been successfully created and tested. Here's a summary of what the regex does:\n\n## Regex Components:\n\n1. **IPv4 Validation**: `(?:25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1[0-9][0-9]|[1-9]?[0-9])`\n   - Matches 0-255 per octet without leading zeros\n   - Uses word boundaries to prevent `a192.168.1.1` or `192.168.1.1a` matches\n\n2. **Date Validation by Month**:\n   - **31-day months** (Jan, Mar, May, Jul, Aug, Oct, Dec): days 01-31\n   - **30-day months** (Apr, Jun, Sep, Nov): days 01-30\n   - **February**: days 01-29 (as specified, allowing Feb 29 without checking leap years)\n\n3. **Last Date Only**: Uses a negative lookahead `(?!.*\\bDATE\\b)` to ensure no additional dates follow\n\n4. **Word Boundaries**: Prevents false positives like `1134-12-1234` or matching dates/IPs adjacent to alphanumeric characters\n\n## Test Results:\nAll 17 test cases pass, including:\n- ✓ Multiple dates on a line (returns only the last)\n- ✓ Lines without IPv4 (not matched)\n- ✓ Invalid dates (Feb 30, Apr 31) rejected\n- ✓ Invalid IPv4 formats (leading zeros) rejected\n- ✓ Word boundary enforcement (prevents false positives)\n\nThe regex is saved in `/app/regex.txt` and ready for use with Python's `re.findall(pattern, log_text, re.MULTILINE)`."
